 ],
 "literal": "The earth bound and tied by mountains where hills join / the world of the three kings who claimed common ownership / even to those who ruled without sharing / the measure of their years has indeed ended / the wealth they accumulated has indeed remained / … support / to those who let go of the raft, it is difficult indeed, while companions weep / at the time when life is gathered and snatched away / standing on this bank, crossing over and reaching that bank.",
 "translation": "Even for the three great kings who ruled without sharing this earth, which is bound and tied by mountains where hills join, the measure of their years has ended, and the wealth they accumulated has remained behind. When life is gathered and snatched away, while companions weep, for those who have let go of the raft of virtue, it is indeed difficult to cross over from this bank of life and reach the other bank.",
 "modern": "குன்றுகள் பொருந்திய மலைகளால் பிணைத்துக் கட்டப்பட்ட இந்த உலகத்தை, தங்களுக்கு மட்டுமே உரியதாகப் பொதுமையின்றி ஆண்ட மூவேந்தர்களுக்கும் அவர்களுடைய ஆயுட்காலம் முடிந்துவிட்டது; அவர்கள் சேர்த்து வைத்த செல்வமும் இங்கேயே தங்கிவிட்டது. உறவினர்கள் கதறி அழ, உயிர் உடலை விட்டுப் பிரியும் காலத்தில், நற்செயல் என்னும் புணையை (தெப்பத்தை)க் கைவிட்டவர்களுக்கு, இக்கரையாகிய இவ்வுலகிலிருந்து அக்கரையாகிய அவ்வுலகிற்குச் செல்வது மிகவும் அரிதானதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Kañci tiṇai and Perunkañci tuṟai, which deals with the transience of worldly life and the importance of virtue. The poet Piramaṉār addresses the rulers and people, reminding them that even the mighty three kings (Chera, Chola, and Pandya) who ruled the vast earth bounded by mountains could not take their wealth or years with them when they died. He uses the metaphor of a river crossing, where life is 'this bank' and the afterlife is 'that bank'. The raft (puṇai) represents the virtuous deeds that one must hold onto to cross safely. Without this raft, when death snatches life away amidst the weeping of companions, crossing over becomes impossible.",
